<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=bicycle">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bicycle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pedal cycle, Push-bike</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A bicycle is a pedal-driven, single-track vehicle with two wheels attached to a frame, one behind the other. Its first form, Karl von Drais’s two-wheeled running machine without pedals, was shown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> in 1817, and pedals on an enlarged front wheel were added in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the early 1860s. The upright safety bicycle has changed little since the first chain-driven model of around 1885, and it gave women a mobility they had not had before. Ball bearings, pneumatic tyres, chain-driven sprockets and tension-spoked wheels were first invented for the bicycle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bicycle.jpg" alt="Bicycle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karen Roe from Bury St Edmunds, Suffolk, UK, United Kingdom · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1886_Starley_'Rover'_Safety_Cycle_British_Motor_Museum_09-2016_(29928044262).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The dandy horse, also called the Draisienne or Laufmaschine, was invented by the German Baron Karl von Drais, who introduced it to the public in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> in 1817 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1818. It was the first means of human transport to use only two wheels in tandem and is regarded as the first bicycle, though it had no pedals: the rider sat astride a wooden frame and pushed it along with their feet while steering the front wheel. A Scottish blacksmith, Kirkpatrick MacMillan, may have built the first mechanically propelled two-wheeled vehicle in 1839, although the claim is often disputed.</p><p>In the early 1860s the Frenchmen Pierre Michaux and Pierre Lallement added a mechanical crank drive with pedals to an enlarged front wheel, the velocipede, which was the first to be mass-produced. In 1869 the Scotsman Thomas McCall built a rod-driven velocipede with rear-wheel drive, and Eugène Meyer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> patented a method of tensioning wire spokes that allowed stronger, lighter wheels. The velocipede developed into the penny-farthing, with a metal frame, wire-spoked wheels and solid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> tyres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s first cycle factory grew from the Coventry Sewing Machine Company's order for a Michaux cycle in 1868, which led to the Coventry Model.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A bicycle is a human-powered or motor-assisted, pedal-driven, single-track vehicle with two wheels attached to a frame, one behind the other. The basic shape of the upright or safety bicycle has changed little since the first chain-driven model was developed around 1885. Most have a diamond frame, a truss of two triangles. Historically, women's frames had a top tube joined lower on the seat tube, so that a rider could mount and dismount in a skirt or dress; this is called a step-through or open frame.</p><p>It is the most efficient human-powered means of transport in terms of the energy a person must spend to cover a distance. Up to 99 per cent of the energy the rider puts into the pedals reaches the wheels, though gearing may reduce this by 10 to 15 per cent. Cycling takes about four-fifths less energy than walking the same distance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The bicycle had a considerable effect on society, in both culture and industry. Around the turn of the 20th century it reduced crowding in inner-city tenements by letting workers commute from more spacious suburban homes, and it reduced dependence on horses. It also allowed people to travel into the country for leisure, being three times as energy efficient as walking and three to four times as fast.</p><p>Several components that were essential to the development of the car were first invented for the bicycle: ball bearings, pneumatic tyres, chain-driven sprockets and tension-spoked wheels. The first bicycle sharing scheme was the White Bicycle plan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1965.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bicycle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Everything changed with the rubber boom that followed John Boyd Dunlop’s pneumatic tyre of 1888 and the spread of <b>bicycles</b> and motor cars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
